  X19 the multiplier's operands are swapped  equivalent (proven)
        MULTIPLICATION IS COMMUTATIVE. The swap reaches only the operand mux's DEFAULT arm, which carries a0*b0; the DOT arms that supply a1*b1 and a2*b2 are untouched, and the ALU still receives a0_i and b0_i unswapped. Both operands are sign-extended to 33 bits by the same expression before the multiply, so the products are bit-identical rather than merely numerically equal. This proof was retracted once on the strength of a CONTAMINATED run -- see the note above the mutant -- and restored when a clean run showed it surviving. RE-SCORE THIS IF THE MULTIPLIER EVER TREATS ITS PORTS ASYMMETRICALLY: different widths, a signed/unsigned split, or an accumulate that reads one port twice.
